          "question": "A neuropsychological test assessing executive working memory consists of $k = 6$ items.\nA sample of $n = 500$ clinical participants yields the following item-level variance properties:\n- Sum of individual item variances: $\\sum_{j=1}^6 \\sigma_j^2 = 7.20$.\n- Total composite test score variance: $\\sigma_X^2 = 24.00$.\n\nFurthermore, an alternative psychometrician divides the 6-item test into two 3-item half-tests (odd vs. even items, $X_{\\text{odd}}$ and $X_{\\text{even}}$). The sample Pearson correlation between the two half-tests is $r_{\\text{half}} = 0.65$.\n1. Compute Cronbach's alpha ($\\alpha$) for the full 6-item scale.\n2. Compute the split-half reliability of the full test using the Spearman-Brown Prophecy Formula.\n3. If the test developers want to increase the test's reliability to $\\rho_{\\text{target}} = 0.90$ by adding parallel items, compute the required number of additional items $k_{\\text{new}}$.",
